WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:07.204
This is the rapid-fire track: every answer in this episode, back to back, one breath each.

2
00:00:07.204 --> 00:00:08.877
Definition, example, done.

3
00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:12.225
It is made for Wednesday night and Thursday morning.

4
00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:16.087
If one of these sounds unfamiliar, go back to its own track.

5
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:19.244
<v Examiner>Scenario 1, Mrs Harris.

6
00:00:19.694 --> 00:00:21.514
Scenario one, Mrs Harris.

7
00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:27.412
Salbutamol: a bronchodilator, a beta-2 agonist that relaxes airway smooth muscle.

8
00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:33.092
Assess respiratory rate, wheeze, saturations and heart rate, before and after.

9
00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:39.645
Warfarin: monitor the I N R; report bruising, bleeding gums, blood in urine, black stools.

10
00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:44.596
Polypharmacy: many medicines at once; blister packs and visual aids.

11
00:00:44.596 --> 00:00:47.436
Older adults: decreased renal function.

12
00:00:48.686 --> 00:00:50.030
<v Examiner>Scenario 2, Leo.

13
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.721
Scenario two, Leo.

14
00:00:51.721 --> 00:00:57.582
Paracetamol fifteen milligrams per kilogram times eighteen is two hundred and seventy

15
00:00:57.582 --> 00:00:58.341
milligrams.

16
00:00:58.341 --> 00:01:01.582
Paracetamol is an analgesic and an antipyretic.

17
00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:06.133
Children get lower doses because of an immature liver and kidneys.

18
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:10.959
An allergy is the body treating the drug as an antigen; ask, document,

19
00:01:10.959 --> 00:01:12.338
tell the prescriber.

20
00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:17.027
Amoxicillin: fridge, out of reach, finish the course, use a syringe.

21
00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:19.984
<v Examiner>Scenario 3, Mr Tane.

22
00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.147
Scenario three, Mr Tane.

23
00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:27.358
Controlled drug: under special legal controls to reduce misuse and abuse;

24
00:01:27.358 --> 00:01:30.285
double-locked safe, register, two nurses.

25
00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:34.568
After morphine, monitor respiratory rate, sedation and pain.

26
00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:40.707
G T N is sublingual for speed and to skip first-pass; headache and low blood pressure,

27
00:01:40.707 --> 00:01:41.564
so sit down.

28
00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:45.061
A refusal: respect it, ask why, document, report.

29
00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:47.357
<v Examiner>Paracetamol.

30
00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.552
Paracetamol: analgesic and antipyretic, general sale.

31
00:01:51.552 --> 00:01:53.813
Mild to moderate pain and fever.

32
00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:58.335
Adults up to one gram four- to six-hourly, max four grams a day;

33
00:01:58.335 --> 00:02:01.232
children fifteen milligrams per kilogram.

34
00:02:01.232 --> 00:02:04.624
Overdose harms the liver; the antidote is N A C.

35
00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.945
Watch for hidden paracetamol in other products.

36
00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.177
<v Examiner>Salbutamol.

37
00:02:10.627 --> 00:02:15.737
Salbutamol: a bronchodilator, a short-acting beta-2 agonist, the reliever.

38
00:02:15.737 --> 00:02:20.640
Binds beta-2 receptors, relaxes airway smooth muscle, works in minutes.

39
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.952
Asthma and C O P D.

40
00:02:21.952 --> 00:02:24.576
Side effects: tremor, fast heart rate.

41
00:02:24.576 --> 00:02:28.788
Teach the spacer; needing it more often means see the doctor.

42
00:02:28.788 --> 00:02:33.069
Assess breathing, saturations and heart rate before and after.

43
00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:35.941
<v Examiner>Aspirin, warfarin, heparin.

44
00:02:36.391 --> 00:02:40.631
Antiplatelet: stops platelets sticking, like low-dose aspirin.

45
00:02:40.631 --> 00:02:43.367
Anticoagulant: interferes with clotting.

46
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:47.334
Warfarin by mouth, monitored by I N R, antidote vitamin K.

47
00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:50.138
Heparin by injection, antidote protamine.

48
00:02:50.138 --> 00:02:55.747
Both raise bleeding risk: teach bruising, bleeding gums, blood in urine or stools.

49
00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:58.961
Warfarin interacts with aspirin and amiodarone.

50
00:03:00.211 --> 00:03:02.067
<v Examiner>Beta blockers and GTN.

51
00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:08.241
Beta blockers end in olol, like metoprolol: antagonists at beta-1, slow the heart,

52
00:03:08.241 --> 00:03:09.707
lower blood pressure.

53
00:03:09.707 --> 00:03:13.477
Check pulse and blood pressure first; withhold if low.

54
00:03:13.477 --> 00:03:14.873
Don't stop suddenly.

55
00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:20.946
G T N: nitrate vasodilator, anti-anginal; sublingual for speed and to avoid first-pass;

56
00:03:20.946 --> 00:03:25.344
sit down, expect a headache; call one one one if pain persists.

57
00:03:26.594 --> 00:03:27.639
<v Examiner>The penicillins.

58
00:03:28.089 --> 00:03:34.377
Antimicrobials are the whole family: antibiotics, antifungals, antivirals, antiparasitics.

59
00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:39.965
Penicillins end in cillin, like amoxicillin, and damage the bacterial cell wall.

60
00:03:39.965 --> 00:03:45.903
Ask about allergy before every first dose; antibiotics cause the most drug allergies.

61
00:03:45.903 --> 00:03:47.440
Watch for anaphylaxis.

62
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.697
Finish the course.

63
00:03:49.947 --> 00:03:50.737
<v Examiner>Morphine.

64
00:03:51.187 --> 00:03:56.822
Morphine: an opioid analgesic for severe pain, an agonist, Class B controlled.

65
00:03:56.822 --> 00:04:02.529
Toxic effect: respiratory depression, so monitor respiratory rate and sedation.

66
00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:05.852
Also nausea, constipation, low blood pressure.

67
00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:08.308
Antidote: naloxone, an antagonist.

68
00:04:08.308 --> 00:04:10.259
Tolerance develops quickly.

69
00:04:11.509 --> 00:04:12.767
<v Examiner>Pharmacokinetics.

70
00:04:13.217 --> 00:04:16.991
Pharmacokinetics, what the body does to the drug, A D M E.

71
00:04:16.991 --> 00:04:20.504
Absorption into the blood, mostly the small intestine.

72
00:04:20.504 --> 00:04:23.888
Distribution in the blood, bound to plasma proteins.

73
00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:27.141
Metabolism in the liver, to make it water-soluble.

74
00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:29.418
Elimination, mostly by the kidneys.

75
00:04:29.418 --> 00:04:32.867
First-pass: the liver breaks down an oral drug first.

76
00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:35.404
Half-life: time for the level to halve.

77
00:04:36.654 --> 00:04:37.934
<v Examiner>Pharmacodynamics.

78
00:04:38.384 --> 00:04:42.057
Pharmacodynamics, what the drug does to the body.

79
00:04:42.057 --> 00:04:46.105
Agonist: binds and causes a response, like salbutamol.

80
00:04:46.105 --> 00:04:50.752
Antagonist: binds and blocks, like a beta blocker or naloxone.

81
00:04:50.752 --> 00:04:53.751
Side effect: predictable and unintended.

82
00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:57.798
Adverse drug reaction: unexpected, harmful, report it.

83
00:04:57.798 --> 00:05:00.347
Potency: high potency, lower dose.

84
00:05:00.347 --> 00:05:02.671
Efficacy: the maximum response.

85
00:05:02.671 --> 00:05:04.995
Tolerance: decreasing response.

86
00:05:04.995 --> 00:05:07.918
Prophylaxis: prevention before disease.

87
00:05:09.168 --> 00:05:10.512
<v Examiner>Drug names and the law.

88
00:05:10.962 --> 00:05:16.415
Use the generic name: internationally approved, prevents errors like double-dosing,

89
00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:18.452
and allows the cheapest option.

90
00:05:18.452 --> 00:05:24.233
Legal classes: prescription, restricted or pharmacist-only, pharmacy-only, general sale.

91
00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:29.226
Controlled drugs: Class A never prescribed, B like morphine, C like codeine.

92
00:05:29.226 --> 00:05:30.672
Medsafe checks safety.

93
00:05:30.672 --> 00:05:31.591
Pharmac funds.

94
00:05:32.841 --> 00:05:34.249
<v Examiner>Giving medicines safely.

95
00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:40.862
Eight rights: patient, medication, dose, time, route, refuse, indication, documentation;

96
00:05:40.862 --> 00:05:43.524
plus two checks, expiry and allergies.

97
00:05:43.524 --> 00:05:45.765
They decrease medication errors.

98
00:05:45.765 --> 00:05:51.297
Aged care: photo identity, start low and go slow, liquid if they can't swallow,

99
00:05:51.297 --> 00:05:53.328
whānau and E P A for refusal.

100
00:05:53.328 --> 00:05:55.569
Never leave meds at the bedside.

101
00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:59.561
An error: tell the prescriber, document, incident report.

102
00:06:00.811 --> 00:06:01.857
<v Examiner>Abbreviations.

103
00:06:02.307 --> 00:06:08.725
Abbreviations: O D once, B D twice, T D S three times, Q I D four times a day.

104
00:06:08.725 --> 00:06:10.864
Q four H every four hours.

105
00:06:10.864 --> 00:06:12.345
P R N as required.

106
00:06:12.345 --> 00:06:13.415
Stat at once.

107
00:06:13.415 --> 00:06:17.941
Mane morning, nocte night, A C before meals, P C after.

108
00:06:17.941 --> 00:06:21.808
P O by mouth, S L under the tongue, P R rectal.

109
00:06:21.808 --> 00:06:26.581
E C enteric-coated; S R and C R slow release, never crush.

110
00:06:26.581 --> 00:06:29.707
Only the prescriber changes the route.

111
00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.088
<v Examiner>Calculations.

112
00:06:32.538 --> 00:06:37.984
Calculations: what you want over what you've got, times the volume; convert units first.

113
00:06:37.984 --> 00:06:42.748
Five hundred milligrams from nought point two five gram tablets: two tablets.

114
00:06:42.748 --> 00:06:46.956
Adrenaline nought point three milligrams from one milligram per mil:

115
00:06:46.956 --> 00:06:48.441
nought point three mils.

116
00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:51.226
Weight-based: dose per kilogram times weight.

117
00:06:52.476 --> 00:06:53.415
<v Examiner>The lifespan.

118
00:06:53.865 --> 00:06:59.442
Placental barrier: separates maternal and foetal blood, non-selective, drugs cross.

119
00:06:59.442 --> 00:07:04.079
Teratogenic: alters foetal development, worst in the first trimester.

120
00:07:04.079 --> 00:07:09.387
Children: immature liver and kidneys, weight-based doses, offer simple choices.

121
00:07:09.387 --> 00:07:14.494
Older adults: decreased renal function, polypharmacy, start low and go slow.

122
00:07:15.744 --> 00:07:17.067
<v Examiner>Allergy and vaccines.

123
00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:22.568
Anaphylaxis: noisy or difficult breathing, stridor, wheeze, swollen tongue;

124
00:07:22.568 --> 00:07:24.050
dizziness or collapse.

125
00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:27.889
Treat with I M adrenaline, nought point three milligrams.

126
00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:29.909
Mild allergy is rash and itch.

127
00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:34.220
Herd immunity: most people vaccinated, so everyone is protected.

128
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.530
Live vaccines like M M R: not in pregnancy or immunosuppression.

129
00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.188
<v Examiner>Dependence and addiction.

130
00:07:41.638 --> 00:07:47.586
Dependence: withdrawal symptoms when a drug is stopped abruptly; can be purely medical.

131
00:07:47.586 --> 00:07:52.986
Addiction: a behaviour; overwhelming involvement in getting and using, craving,

132
00:07:52.986 --> 00:07:54.764
use despite harm, relapse.

133
00:07:54.764 --> 00:07:57.635
Tolerance: less effect from the same dose.

134
00:07:57.635 --> 00:08:00.027
Licit is legal, illicit is illegal.

135
00:08:00.027 --> 00:08:03.035
Naloxone for opioids; N A C for paracetamol.

136
00:08:04.285 --> 00:08:05.053
<v Examiner>Adherence.

137
00:08:05.503 --> 00:08:09.541
Adherence: taking the medicine as agreed with the prescriber.

138
00:08:09.541 --> 00:08:11.064
Compliance is outdated.

139
00:08:11.064 --> 00:08:15.565
Barriers: no transport to the pharmacy, cost, not understanding why,

140
00:08:15.565 --> 00:08:18.080
can't open the packaging, a busy life.

141
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.589
"I can't afford it": social worker and every subsidy.

142
00:08:22.839 --> 00:08:24.311
<v Examiner>Complementary medicine.

143
00:08:24.761 --> 00:08:30.348
Complementary: alongside conventional medicine, like an osteopath with medication.

144
00:08:30.348 --> 00:08:32.187
Alternative: instead of it.

145
00:08:32.187 --> 00:08:37.911
If someone plans to stop treatment, explore why, inform, involve the team, document,

146
00:08:37.911 --> 00:08:39.206
respect the choice.

147
00:08:39.206 --> 00:08:44.793
Rongoā Māori: mirimiri, karakia and rongoā rākau; the practitioner is responsible.

148
00:08:46.043 --> 00:08:47.152
<v Examiner>Drug development.

149
00:08:47.602 --> 00:08:53.079
Clinical trial ethics: informed consent, purpose, benefits and risks before agreeing;

150
00:08:53.079 --> 00:08:55.528
and the right to withdraw at any time.

151
00:08:55.528 --> 00:09:00.618
Phases: preclinical in the lab; one, healthy volunteers, safety; two, patients,

152
00:09:00.618 --> 00:09:06.418
does it work; three, large groups, better than current; four, post-marketing surveillance.

153
00:09:06.418 --> 00:09:09.639
Bioequivalent: same rate and extent of absorption.

154
00:09:10.889 --> 00:09:12.340
<v Examiner>Her keys and the classes.

155
00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:17.310
The classes both ways: anti-emetic stops nausea and vomiting;

156
00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:23.756
antihistamine blocks histamine; bronchodilator opens airways; diuretic increases urine;

157
00:09:23.756 --> 00:09:27.534
analgesic relieves pain; antipyretic reduces fever.

158
00:09:27.534 --> 00:09:30.202
Enteric coating resists the stomach.

159
00:09:30.202 --> 00:09:32.498
Most drugs leave by the kidney.

160
00:09:32.498 --> 00:09:34.795
Topical goes on a body surface.

161
00:09:34.795 --> 00:09:36.573
Medsafe monitors safety.

162
00:09:36.573 --> 00:09:39.167
Always and never are usually wrong.

163
00:09:40.417 --> 00:09:41.554
That's every answer.

164
00:09:41.554 --> 00:09:42.465
Go and get them.
